 "title": "Byzantine Empire",
 "variantOf": "Q12544",
 "endonym": {
  "native": "Βασιλεία Ῥωμαίων",
  "roman": "Basileía Rhōmaíōn"
 },
 "capsule": "As the Arab conquests stripped away Syria, Palestine, and Egypt after 633, the empire reorganized around a system of military provinces (themes) and endured centuries of contraction and recovery, from Herakleios's reforms through the Macedonian dynasty's 9th-11th century resurgence and the Komnenian restoration. Constantinople fell to the Fourth Crusade in 1204, and though the Palaiologan dynasty recovered the city in 1261 and ruled a much-reduced state until the Ottoman conquest of 1453, a last Byzantine Greek remnant, the Principality of Theodoro in Crimea, survived until the Ottomans overran it in 1475.",
 "citations": [
